<BrandGuide
  brandKey="gap"
  brand="Gap & Old Navy"
  title="Gap & Old Navy Size Chart: Do They Run Big?"
  description="Gap and Old Navy tend to run big, with Old Navy the roomier of the two. Fit notes by category, size conversions, and when to size down in stretch denim."
  fitSections={fitSections}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <Fragment slot="verdict">
    <strong>Gap and Old Navy tend to run big.</strong> Both are classic American vanity-sized
    brands cut with generous ease, and Old Navy is commonly reported as the roomier of the two.
    Most shoppers can take their usual US size at Gap, while many go one size down at Old Navy —
    especially in stretch denim, which relaxes further with wear.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <li><strong>Treat the two brands separately:</strong> a Gap medium and an <AffLink brand="oldnavy">Old Navy</AffLink> medium are cut from different blocks; many shoppers wear a size smaller at Old Navy.</li>
    <li><strong>Buy stretch denim snug:</strong> jeans with stretch commonly loosen at the waist and seat within a few wears, so take the tighter of two sizes.</li>
    <li><strong>Between sizes? Go down:</strong> with generous vanity sizing, the smaller size is usually the safer bet at both brands.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the fit name, not just the size:</strong> both brands sell the same size across slim, straight, and relaxed cuts, and the cut changes the fit more than one size step does.</li>
  </Fragment>
</BrandGuide>
